    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. can u explain how this actually works on a cellular level? like what is happening with the insulin when we eat those high gi foods compared to the slow ones?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When you consume high GI foods, your pancreas releases a large burst of insulin quickly to drive glucose rapidly into your cells, often causing cellular inflammation and resistance over time. With low GI foods, insulin is secreted more gradually, allowing cells to take up glucose steadily without triggering that overpowering surge. This sustained mechanism helps your cells, especially in muscles and fat tissue, remain more sensitive to insulin, which is crucial for managing Type 2 diabetes long-term.",
      "source": "target",
